    content: `## Summary: The Culture Behind Corporate Japan
In Japanese corporate culture (*Nihon no Bijinesu Shūkan*), technical competence is respected, but adherence to business etiquette (*Reigi Sahō*) builds lasting trust. Knowing how to exchange business cards, phrase polite requests, and draft formal business correspondence sets international professionals apart immediately.

---

### The Three Tiers of Keigo (敬語)

Japanese features three distinct levels of formal language:

| Tier of Keigo | Japanese Name | Purpose & Who it Applies to | Everyday Verb | Keigo Transformation |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Polite Speech** | *Teineigo* (丁寧語) | Standard polite speech ending in *desu/masu*. Used with peers and acquaintances. | 行く (*Iku*) | 行きます (*Ikimasu*) |
| **Respectful Speech** | *Sonkeigo* (尊敬語) | Elevates the actions of clients, superiors, and business partners. Never used for yourself. | 行く (*Iku*) | **いらっしゃいます** (*Irasshaimasu*) |
| **Humble Speech** | *Kenjōgo* (謙譲語) | Lowers your own actions to express humility before clients and executives. | 行く (*Iku*) | **参ります** (*Mairimasu*) |

---

### The Sacred Art of Meishi Kōkan (名刺交換 - Business Card Exchange)

When meeting Japanese clients or partners for the first time, business cards are exchanged before any discussion begins:

1. **Holding the Card**: Always present and receive the business card with **both hands**, holding it by the top corners so you don't cover the company logo or name.
2. **Bow and Introduction**: Bow slightly (15–30 degrees) and state your company and full name:  
   *「富士日本語アカデミーの〇〇と申します。どうぞよろしくお願い申し上げます。」*
3. **Card Placement on the Table**: Never put a received card into your pocket or wallet during the meeting. Place it neatly on top of your card case on the table, aligned with the person sitting across from you.`,
