    content: `## Summary: The Phonic Bedrock of Japanese
Japanese literacy begins with mastering the two phonetic kana alphabets: **Hiragana (ひらがな)** for native grammar and particles, and **Katakana (カタカナ)** for foreign loanwords and technical vocabulary.

Both scripts represent the exact same 46 phonetic sounds arranged in an intuitive grid of 5 vowels (a, i, u, e, o) combined with 9 consonant stems.

---

### The 46-Kana Matrix Grid

- **A-Row (あ行)**: あ (a), い (i), う (u), え (e), お (o)
- **Ka-Row (か行)**: か (ka), き (ki), く (ku), け (ke), こ (ko)
- **Sa-Row (さ行)**: さ (sa), し (shi), す (su), せ (se), そ (so)
- **Ta-Row (た行)**: た (ta), ち (chi), つ (tsu), て (te), と (to)
- **Na-Row (な行)**: な (na), に (ni), ぬ (nu), ね (ne), の (no)
- **Ha-Row (は行)**: は (ha), ひ (hi), ふ (fu), へ (he), ほ (ho)
- **Ma-Row (ま行)**: ま (ma), み (mi), む (mu), め (me), も (mo)
- **Ya-Row (や行)**: や (ya), ゆ (yu), よ (yo)
- **Ra-Row (ら行)**: ら (ra), り (ri), る (ru), れ (re), ろ (ro)
- **Wa-Row (わ行)**: わ (wa), を (wo/o), ん (n)`,
